    {
      "question": "e-12467",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "45217202fa720449b1b1274dc6502467e433b4e97c3a9cf9db922d574dfdb071",
      "new_sha256": "9a41bc37465a2e36f91825dd1cb8acd213e7c4118ca29a0dc77511f44555445d",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-12467",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(2{y}^{2}+{y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{-2}) \\\\  & =4y-2{y}^{-3} \\\\  & =4y-\\frac{2}{{y}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{4}-2}{{y}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{y^3}{4y^4 -2}.\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977687/example-12467.json",
          "file_sha256": "ff0abd0441ed4667c164a6baa1b3a73544eebdd5dd79e5945c5f97b3976cdaf4"
        }
