    {
      "question": "e-12468",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "9c7d6fbac2533bb1e92f73e49c6b1ca4af74cad02579e5022ab665c5431ff6c5",
      "new_sha256": "a8860b54496a9dc916e6cd6d0fcad6fb378f1b06a4d2fda91369c2d7fe483963",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-12468",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "First, we differentiate $x(y)$ with respect to $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+2y\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y+2{y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(y)+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =1+2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =1+3{y}^{1/2} \\\\  & =1+3\\sqrt{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, taking the reciprocal of $\\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm dx} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm dx}{\\textrm dy} \\right)^{-1} =  \\dfrac{1}{1 + 3\\sqrt y}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the point $(3,1),$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}{|}_{(x,y)=(3,1)} & =\\frac{1}{1+3\\sqrt{1}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977687/example-12468.json",
          "file_sha256": "e503f6cbc77f219104a634963cd6c430aa4fa5bfdc2ae6614282196fbe9d1356"
